# Settings for the llama.cpp add-ons (docker/options/llama-*.yml).
#
#   cp .env.llama.example .env.llama
#
# One file for all five hardware variants — they differ only in image and
# device wiring, never in configuration. Read by the llama container.
#
# LLAMA_ARG_* are llama-server's own variable names. That is deliberate:
# compose only interpolates ${...} from the root .env, never from a scoped
# file, so a scoped file can only configure a service through the names that
# service already understands. The services therefore pass no command-line
# arguments at all, and everything below takes effect.

# The model to serve, as a path inside the container. Put the .gguf file in the
# directory LLAMA_MODELS_DIR points at (default: models/ at the repo root) —
# it is mounted read-only at /models — and name it here. There is no sensible
# default; the server exits if this file is missing.
LLAMA_ARG_MODEL=/models/your-model.gguf

# Layers to offload onto the GPU. 99 means "all of them" and is what you want
# on any of the GPU variants; delete this line for the CPU variant.
LLAMA_ARG_N_GPU_LAYERS=99

# Context window in tokens. Transcripts get long, so the 4096 default is often
# too small; raise it until you run out of memory.
LLAMA_ARG_CTX_SIZE=8192

# Optional: CPU threads. Defaults to the container's core count.
# LLAMA_ARG_THREADS=8

# Do not set LLAMA_ARG_HOST or LLAMA_ARG_PORT. The image already listens on
# 0.0.0.0:8080, and the compose port mapping and healthcheck both assume it.

# ── The other half of the pairing lives in the root .env ──────────
# These two tell SupoClip to *use* the server, and they must go in .env rather
# than here: compose passes them into the backend and worker through its
# `environment:` block, which always outranks any env_file. Set them there:
#
#   LLM=openai:local
#   OPENAI_BASE_URL=http://llama:8080/v1
#
# The model name after `openai:` is arbitrary — llama.cpp serves whichever
# model you loaded above, whatever you call it.
